        </ul>
        <h3>What Makes an Opal Valuable?</h3>
        <h4>Play-of-Colour (Most Important)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Distinct, lively spectrum that shifts with movement</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Well-defined colour flashes — never flat, static, or dull</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Cloudy or muted play-of-colour may indicate poor quality or treatment</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Opals with no visible play-of-colour should be carefully examined</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Base Tone</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Milky to white base — classic and traditionally preferred</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Grey, blue, black, or green tones — equally beautiful and often more valuable</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Black and blue opals are the rarest and most premium</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Surface & Finish</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Smooth polish with an even surface</strong></li>
          <li><strong>No visible cracks, pits, or chalky texture</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Well-proportioned dome with balanced shaping for durability</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Origin & Stability</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Australian opals are renowned for stability, clarity, and long-term fire</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Ethiopian opals are visually striking but hydrophane, meaning they may cloud when exposed to moisture</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Origin directly affects durability, care requirements, and long-term appearance</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Avoid Doublets & Triplets (Critical)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Doublets: Thin opal layer bonded to a backing</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Triplets: Thin opal layer sandwiched between a backing and a clear cap</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>These are less durable, significantly lower in value, and are not considered solid natural opal gemstones.</strong></p>
        <p><strong>Always confirm the opal is solid and natural through proper certification.</strong></p>
