        <h4>Certification Checklist</h4>
        <h4>Ensure the certificate includes</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Verification from a credible third-party laboratory</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Confirmation that the stone is a natural opal</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Explicit mention that it is not a doublet or triplet</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Disclosure of any wax, resin, or filler treatment</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Clear origin declaration (Australian or Ethiopian)</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Hydrophane disclosure for Ethiopian opals</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Why Some Opals Are Suspiciously Cheap</h3>
        <h4>Low-priced opals are often</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Doublets or triplets misrepresented as solid opals</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Enhanced using wax or resin to improve appearance</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Sold with misleading origin claims</strong></li>
          <li><strong>Ethiopian opals falsely marketed as Australian</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>True value lies in natural character, authenticity, transparency, and long-term durability.</strong></p>
