    {%- when 'ruby' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--ruby">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>All our Rubies are certified natural and genuine. Every stone comes with full treatment disclosure, independent third-party lab verification, and a certificate verifiable online. No glass filling, no lead filling, no diffusion treatment — ever. Astrological guidance is available if you need help assessing suitability before purchasing.</p>
        <p><strong>What makes a Ruby valuable? Colour is the single most important factor — look for a balanced, natural red with genuine inner glow. The finest stones are described as </strong>Pigeon Blood<strong> or </strong>Vivid Red: luminous, rich, and alive. Extremely dark or brownish-red stones can feel heavy; overly bright neon reds often indicate excessive treatment. Fine silk inclusions — the natural internal texture of a genuine Ruby — are desirable, not a flaw. A completely glassy, flawless-looking stone deserves careful scrutiny. Cut should be even and proportioned, with no surface-reaching fractures or filled cavities.</p>
        <p><strong>How to verify authenticity: Request an independent third-party lab certificate confirming the stone is a natural </strong>Ruby (Corundum), stating the treatment type clearly, confirming no glass or lead filling, and providing an origin opinion for premium stones. The certificate should be verifiable online. Avoid in-house reports and any seller who cannot clearly answer questions about treatment or origin.</p>
        <p>Why are some Rubies suspiciously cheap? Almost always: glass or lead filling disguising poor clarity, heavy or unstable treatment, misrepresented origin, or a combination of all three. True Ruby value lives in natural colour, structural integrity, and honest disclosure — not in how convincing the price looks.</p>
      </div>
