    {%- when 'red-coral' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--red_coral">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Red Coral is one of the most widely misrepresented gemstones in the market. Ceramic, plastic, resin composite, dyed, and reconstructed coral are all commonly sold — sometimes deliberately misrepresented, sometimes through genuine ignorance in the supply chain. An informed purchase protects both astrological suitability and long-term value.</p>
        <p><strong>Natural versus dyed</strong> is the single most important distinction. Natural coral shows soft, earthy red tones with an organic warmth. Dyed coral looks unnaturally bright, overly uniform, or artificially glossy — and the dye may fade or bleed with wear. Dyed or reconstructed coral has no value for Mangal remedies and should be strictly avoided.</p>
        <p><strong>What to look for in a genuine stone:</strong> Deep, even red to vermilion colour with natural warmth, not artificial shine. A dense, compact feel with fine, tight grain — chalky, porous, or lightweight coral wears quickly and signals poor quality. A smooth cabochon with even curvature and a silky polish, not glassy. No pits, cracks, or uneven surfaces. Treatments to avoid completely include reconstituted coral made from powder and resin, plastic-bonded composites, dyed material, and any resin-filled stone.</p>
        <p><strong>Common imitations</strong> include ceramic coral, plastic and resin copies, and powder-resin composites. Red flags are overly uniform colour, a glassy or plastic feel, unusually light weight, and unrealistically low prices.</p>
        <p><strong>All our Red Coral is natural, untreated, and ocean-origin</strong> — selected specifically for density, durability, and astrological suitability, with complete disclosure and third-party certification verifiable online.</p>
        <p><strong>How to verify authenticity:</strong> Request an independent lab certificate confirming natural coral, no dye or reconstruction, and clear origin disclosure. The certificate must be verifiable online. Avoid any seller who cannot provide this or who is vague about whether the material is solid natural coral.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'pearl' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--pearl">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Pearl is widely imitated and frequently misrepresented. Plastic pearls, glass beads with coating, shell composites, and ceramic imitations are all commonly sold — and some are convincing enough to deceive buyers who do not know what to look for. An informed purchase protects both the investment and the astrological purpose.</p>
        <p><strong>Lustre is the truest measure of a Pearl's quality.</strong> Look for a soft, deep, moon-like glow that seems to come from within the stone rather than sitting on its surface. Dull or chalky surfaces indicate poor nacre quality. Overly sharp or metallic shine often signals coating or artificial treatment. A genuine pearl's light is warm and diffused, never harsh.</p>
        <p><strong>Nacre thickness</strong> determines how long that lustre lasts. Thick nacre wears and ages gracefully. Thin nacre dulls quickly and is a sign of lower quality regardless of how attractive the pearl looks at the point of purchase.</p>
        <p><strong>Colour</strong> should be natural — white, cream, or soft ivory. Dyed pinks, greys, or unnaturally uniform tones are a red flag. A pearl that bleeds or stains when gently wiped has been dyed and is not suitable for astrological use.</p>
        <p><strong>Surface quality</strong> in a natural pearl will show minor marks and irregularities — this is normal and expected. What is not acceptable is deep cracking, peeling, or a flaky texture, which indicates poor quality or imitation material.</p>
        <p><strong>All our Pearls are selected for lustre depth and nacre quality,</strong> fully disclosed, independently certified, and free from artificial shine, dye, or cosmetic enhancement. Every certification is from a credible third-party lab and verifiable online.</p>
        <p><strong>How to verify authenticity:</strong> Request an independent lab certificate confirming natural or cultured Pearl, clearly disclosing any dyeing or treatments, with online verification available. Avoid any seller who cannot provide this.</p>
        <p><strong>Why are some Pearls very cheap?</strong> Almost always because they are plastic, glass, or shell imitations with surface coating, or genuine but thin-nacre cultured pearls that will dull quickly. True Pearl value lives in lustre depth, nacre thickness, natural origin, and honest disclosure.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'hessonite' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--hessonite">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Hessonite works through warmth, depth, and internal glow — not through sparkle or surface flash. That distinction matters when buying, because the qualities that make Hessonite astrologically valuable are not the same ones that make a stone look impressive under a jewellery store spotlight.</p>
        <p><strong>Colour is the most important factor.</strong> Look for warm, earthy tones — honey, amber, cinnamon, cognac — with a soft, calm glow that comes from within. Very dark or muddy brown stones feel heavy and lifeless. Pale, washed-out yellow stones lack presence. An artificially bright or glassy appearance is a red flag for treatment or misrepresentation.</p>
        <p><strong>Internal glow and texture</strong> matter as much as surface appearance. An even, calm internal glow with natural soft reflections is what to look for. A completely glassy appearance or a flat, dead look signals either heavy treatment or poor quality. Minor natural inclusions are entirely normal and should not concern a careful buyer — overall presence matters more than perfection.</p>
        <p><strong>Shape and cut</strong> for astrological use should favour smooth cabochons with even symmetry and polish. Avoid sharp edges and uneven or distorted domes, which compromise both appearance and durability.</p>
        <p><strong>Treatment status</strong> should always be clearly disclosed. Untreated Hessonite is preferred for Rahu remedies. Light heat treatment is acceptable when disclosed. Heavy treatments, glass filling, and diffusion are not acceptable under any circumstances.</p>
        <p><strong>All our Hessonite is selected for colour warmth and internal glow</strong> — chosen for astrological integrity, free from artificial brilliance or hidden enhancement, fully disclosed, and independently certified with online-verifiable reports.</p>
        <p><strong>How to verify authenticity:</strong> Request an independent third-party lab certificate confirming natural Hessonite garnet, clearly stating treatment type, with no glass filling or diffusion, and verifiable online. Avoid any seller who cannot provide this or who is vague about treatment status or origin.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is some Hessonite very cheap?</strong> Almost always: poor colour, dull or flat appearance, synthetic or misrepresented material, or undisclosed treatments. True Hessonite value lives in colour warmth, internal depth, and honest disclosure.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'cats-eye' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--cats_eye">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Cat's Eye works through precision, not power — and buying it requires the same quality of attention. The eye effect alone is not sufficient. Material, sharpness, contrast, and honest disclosure together determine whether a stone has genuine astrological value or merely a visual effect.</p>
        <p><strong>Material is the most important factor.</strong> Chrysoberyl is the true Lehsunia. Quartz is a secondary option with limited chart-specific use. A moving line alone — however striking — does not make a stone suitable for Ketu. Always confirm the base mineral through independent certification before purchasing.</p>
        <p><strong>Eye quality</strong> is what separates a valuable stone from a merely attractive one. Look for a sharp, straight, well-centred eye that runs continuously from end to end and is clearly visible in normal light. Broken, diffused, or double lines indicate poor quality. Strong contrast between the bright eye and the body colour is essential — a washed-out or low-contrast eye lacks the precision that makes this stone what it is.</p>
        <p><strong>Cut matters more than with most stones.</strong> A proper cabochon cut is essential — smooth, symmetrical dome, with the eye sitting exactly across the centre. Poor cutting weakens or misaligns the eye regardless of the stone's inherent quality.</p>
        <p><strong>Treatments and imitations</strong> are widespread in this category. Dyed stones, glass and resin composites, fibre-optic synthetics, and low-quality Quartz sold as Chrysoberyl are all commonly encountered. None of these carry astrological value. Always insist on independent third-party certification confirming the base material.</p>
        <p><strong>All our Cat's Eye is natural, disclosed, and selected for material authenticity and eye precision</strong> — astrologically suitable, never cosmetic-only, fully certified and verifiable online.</p>
        <p><strong>How to verify authenticity:</strong> Request an independent lab certificate confirming natural Chrysoberyl or Quartz, clearly stating treatment type, with no glass filling or synthetic material, verifiable online. For a stone in this price range and with this level of astrological significance, certification is not optional.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is some Cat's Eye very cheap?</strong> Almost always: Quartz sold as Chrysoberyl, synthetic or fibre-optic material, poor eye quality, or undisclosed treatments. The difference in astrological value between a sharp, natural Chrysoberyl and a glass composite with a convincing eye effect is total — not a matter of degree.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'amethyst' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--amethyst">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>A good Amethyst should feel composed, never flashy. The qualities that make it valuable are the same qualities it is worn to cultivate — calm depth, even clarity, and a presence that does not need to announce itself.</p>
        <p>Colour is the most important factor. Look for a rich, even purple in the range from soft violet to deep royal — balanced saturation that feels alive without being aggressive. Too pale and the stone lacks presence; too dark or muddy and it turns flat. Gentle natural colour zoning is acceptable and expected. Harsh banding or patchy, uneven colour lowers quality and value.</p>
        <p>Clarity in Amethyst is generally good — most natural stones are eye-clean, with smooth light flow and good transparency. Visible cracks, cloudy patches, or dull areas are signs of poor quality.</p>
        <p>Cut matters more than it is sometimes given credit for. Even facets, good symmetry, and a cut that enhances the depth of the purple — oval, cushion, round, and emerald cuts all work well. Poor cutting dulls the colour regardless of how good the rough was.</p>
        <p>On treatments: Most natural Amethyst is untreated — this is one of the cleaner categories in the gemstone market. The primary thing to be aware of is heat treatment, which can turn Amethyst yellow and lead to its being sold as Citrine. Always confirm the stone is natural quartz and that no treatment has been applied or concealed.</p>
        <p>All our Amethyst is selected for natural colour and clarity — no artificial dyeing, no misrepresentation, fully disclosed and quality-checked.</p>
        <p>How to verify authenticity: Request a third-party certificate confirming natural Amethyst (Quartz), with clear treatment disclosure. For a stone at this price point it is a straightforward ask — any reputable seller should provide it without hesitation.</p>
        <p>Why is some Amethyst very cheap? Pale or low-grade colour, poor cutting, or imitation and misrepresented material. The price range for Amethyst is wide, and the lower end of that range reflects quality that is unlikely to serve astrological purposes well. Choose presence and clarity over carat weight or price.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'citrine' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--citrine">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>A well-chosen Citrine should feel warm, balanced, and quietly radiant. The qualities that make it valuable are the same ones it is worn to support — clarity, warmth, and a grounded brightness that does not need to perform.</p>
        <p>Colour is the most important factor. Look for a natural, even yellow to golden hue — lemon, warm honey, or gentle gold — with a warmth that feels organic rather than artificial. Too pale and the stone lacks presence. Brownish or muddy tones indicate poor quality. The glow should come from within the stone, not sit on its surface.</p>
        <p>Clarity and transparency are generally strong in natural Citrine — most good stones are eye-clean, allowing light to move freely through them. Cloudiness, haze, or milky patches signal poor quality or treatment.</p>
        <p>Cut matters significantly. Well-proportioned cuts spread the stone's warmth evenly and bring out its natural glow. Poor cutting makes even good colour look flat and lifeless. Oval, cushion, round, and emerald cuts all work well for this stone.</p>
        <p>On treatments and imitations: Most natural Citrine is untreated — it is one of the cleaner categories in the market. The primary misrepresentation to be aware of is heat-treated Amethyst being sold as Citrine, and dyed quartz or coloured glass sold as natural Citrine. Always confirm the stone is natural quartz with clear treatment disclosure. A good stone should have an honest, non-artificial brilliance — depth of colour that comes from nature, not chemistry.</p>
        <p>All our Citrine is selected for warm natural colour and clarity — no misrepresentation, no artificial exaggeration, fully disclosed and quality-checked.</p>
        <p>How to verify authenticity: Request a credible independent lab certificate confirming natural Citrine, with any treatment clearly disclosed. At this price point it is a straightforward ask and should be provided without hesitation.</p>
        <p>Why is some Citrine very cheap? Pale or low-grade colour, poor cutting, or dyed quartz and imitation material. The difference between a well-selected natural Citrine and a pale, flat substitute is visible — and meaningful for anyone wearing it with a specific purpose in mind.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'turquoise' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--turquoise">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Turquoise is one of the most widely imitated and misrepresented gemstones available. Plastic and resin moulds, ceramic and composite material, reconstituted powder-and-binder stones, and dyed howlite sold as Turquoise are all common — and some are convincing enough to deceive buyers who do not know what distinguishes the real thing.</p>
        <p>Colour should be natural, even, and alive — sky blue, robin's-egg blue, or soft blue-green with a warmth that feels organic. Neon or painted-looking colour, and glassy surface shine, are both red flags for dye or artificial treatment. True Turquoise colour has depth, not just brightness.</p>
        <p>Texture should feel fine and compact — a natural, stone-like presence. Chalky or powdery material indicates poor quality. Overly glossy surfaces often signal resin coating or plastic filling that is concealing what lies beneath.</p>
        <p>Matrix — the natural veining and patterning within the stone — is part of Turquoise's character. Minimal matrix is preferred in classic Iranian stones; decorative spiderweb matrix is valued in certain American stones. Painted-looking or artificial lines are a clear sign of imitation.</p>
        <p>Imitation red flags to watch for: stones that look too perfect, too uniform in colour, or too large for the price being asked. Identical patterns across multiple stones in a collection, and a warm or slightly sticky feel, both suggest resin composite or plastic. True Turquoise carries character — variation, texture, and a naturalness that cannot be fully replicated.</p>
        <p>Natural versus stabilised: Natural, untreated Turquoise is rare and commands a premium. Stabilised Turquoise — resin-impregnated for durability — is widely available and entirely acceptable when disclosed. What is never acceptable is stabilisation or dyeing presented as natural, or plastic and ceramic sold as stone.</p>
        <p>All our Turquoise is selected for colour integrity and natural feel — treatment fully disclosed, no plastic, ceramic, or reconstituted misrepresentation, chosen for authenticity rather than spectacle.</p>
        <p>How to verify authenticity: Request a credible third-party lab certificate confirming natural or stabilised Turquoise with treatment clearly stated and origin disclosed. For a stone this widely imitated, independent certification is the only reliable protection.</p>
        <p>Why is some Turquoise very cheap? Almost always: plastic or resin imitation, reconstituted powder composite, dyed substitute material, or heavily stabilised stone presented as natural. If a Turquoise looks too perfect, too vivid, too large, or too inexpensive — be cautious. Genuine Turquoise carries natural character, and natural character has a price.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'lapis-lazuli' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--lapis_lazuli">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Lapis Lazuli is widely admired and just as widely imitated, dyed, and misrepresented. Dyed howlite, dyed sodalite, glass composites, and powder-bound synthetic stones are all commonly sold in its place — some convincingly enough to deceive buyers who do not know what distinguishes genuine material.</p>
        <p>Colour should be deep royal or ultramarine blue with a natural warmth and variation. Artificially bright or electric blue signals dye. A colour that bleeds or leaves residue when the stone is wiped confirms treatment. True Lapis colour has depth, not just saturation.</p>
        <p>Calcite — the white mineral that naturally occurs within Lapis — should be minimal in quality stones. Heavy white patches lower both visual depth and value. Some calcite is natural and expected; dominance of it is not desirable.</p>
        <p>Pyrite — the golden flecks — should be fine and evenly dispersed. When balanced, pyrite is one of the stone's most beautiful features. When it dominates or appears in large, uneven clusters, it detracts from the overall quality.</p>
        <p>Texture should feel dense and substantial — a solid, grounded presence. Chalky, porous, or crumbly material indicates poor quality. An overly glossy or unnaturally uniform surface often signals resin coating or composite material.</p>
        <p>Always ask clearly: Is this stone natural, dyed, or colour-enhanced? A genuine Lapis seller will answer without hesitation. If a stone looks too bright, too perfect, too uniform, or too inexpensive — pause. True Lapis carries character, variation, and a naturalness that imitations cannot fully replicate.</p>
        <p>How to verify authenticity: Request an independent lab certificate confirming natural Lapis Lazuli with treatment status clearly stated. Look for dense, substantial feel, natural colour variation, and no blue residue or colour bleeding when wiped. Origin should be disclosed — Afghan Badakhshan remains the benchmark.</p>
        <p>Why is some Lapis very cheap? Dyed howlite, dyed sodalite, glass, or reconstituted powder composites — almost always. Quality Lapis with genuine depth, minimal calcite, and balanced pyrite is not a cheap stone. If the price does not reflect that, the material probably does not either.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'tourmaline' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--tourmaline">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Colour is everything in Tourmaline. Look for pure, well-balanced colour with natural depth and life — a saturation that feels genuine rather than chemical. Stones that are too dark or muddy lose their expressiveness; those that appear neon or artificially vivid often indicate enhancement or misrepresentation. The colour should feel composed, not exaggerated.</p>
        <p>Clarity and cut both matter significantly. Good transparency allows light to move freely through the stone and reveals the colour at its best. A cut that supports even colour flow — avoiding the dark zones and windowing that come from poor proportions — is essential. Heavy cloudiness and surface-reaching fractures are signs of poor quality.</p>
        <p>Treatment disclosure should always be confirmed. Most Tourmaline is untreated, and any seller representing a stone as such should be able to support that claim with independent certification for higher-value pieces. Avoid artificial coatings, vague seller claims, and any stone where the colour looks too perfect or too uniform to feel natural.</p>
        <p>On colour alternatives: If you are drawn to Tourmaline as a gentler alternative to a primary planetary stone — Green Tourmaline for Mercury, Rubellite for the Sun — be clear with yourself about the purpose. Tourmaline is a considered, respected choice in these roles, but it is not a direct substitute for the primary stone. The gentleness is both its virtue and its limit.</p>
        <p>How to verify authenticity: Request a credible independent lab certificate confirming natural Tourmaline with treatment status clearly disclosed. For higher-value stones — fine Indicolite, Rubellite, or rare bi-colour material — certification is non-negotiable.</p>
        <p>Why is some Tourmaline very cheap? Poor colour, dull clarity, low-grade material, or misrepresented treatment. The price range for Tourmaline is genuinely wide — but within that range, quality is clearly visible to an informed eye.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'aquamarine' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--aquamarine">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>A good Aquamarine should feel clear, composed, and effortless — a blue that opens rather than closes, and a transparency that lets light move freely through the stone.</p>
        <p>Colour should be a clean blue to blue-green — balanced and calm, neither too pale to carry presence nor too dark to retain the openness that defines this stone. The ideal sits somewhere in the range from soft sky-blue through medium ocean-blue, with the finest Santa Maria quality stones offering a deeper, richer tone that still holds transparency. Too pale and the stone washes out; too dark and it loses the quality that makes Aquamarine what it is.</p>
        <p>Transparency and clarity are non-negotiable. Aquamarine is valued specifically for its open, clear visual character — heavy inclusions, cloudiness, or milky patches represent a fundamental loss of the stone's defining quality. Most quality Aquamarine is eye-clean. If it is not, the price should reflect that and the reason should be clear.</p>
        <p>Cut matters significantly. Poor proportions create windowing — a see-through, colourless area in the centre of the stone — which undermines both beauty and value. A well-cut stone spreads colour evenly and allows light to interact with the stone rather than pass straight through it.</p>
        <p>How to verify authenticity: Request an independent lab certificate confirming natural Aquamarine (Beryl) with treatment status clearly disclosed. For higher-value stones — particularly anything represented as Santa Maria quality — certification from a recognised gem lab is essential. Santa Maria is a quality descriptor, not a protected origin designation, and it is sometimes used loosely.</p>
        <p>Why is some Aquamarine very cheap? Pale or low-grade colour, poor cutting that creates significant windowing, heavy inclusions, or synthetic and glass substitutes. The wide price range reflects genuine differences in quality — the lower end of that range typically represents stones that lack the transparency and colour presence that make Aquamarine worth wearing.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'pitambari' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--pitambari">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Most Important: Natural Bi-Colour Formation</p>
        <p>A true Peetambari must show naturally formed yellow and blue within one crystal.</p>
        <p>Always check for:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural internal bi-colour structure</li>
        <li>No diffusion treatment</li>
        <li>Balanced, harmonious zoning</li>
        <li>Good colour stability</li>
        <li>Verified third-party certification</li>
        <li>Clear treatment disclosure</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Strictly Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Diffusion-treated stones</li>
        <li>Artificial colour enhancement</li>
        <li>Patchy or unstable colour</li>
        <li>No certification</li>
        <li>“Bi-colour” claims without proof</li>
        <li>️ Diffusion-treated Peetambari is not suitable for astrological use.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A true Peetambari does not look dramatic —</p>
        <p>it feels balanced.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'purple-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--purple_sapphire">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Balanced violet or wine-purple colour</li>
        <li>Depth with visible light return (not blacked out)</li>
        <li>No glass filling or diffusion treatment</li>
        <li>Clear treatment disclosure</li>
        <li>Verified third-party certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Overly red or overly blue stones (loss of balance)</li>
        <li>Inky, muddy, or lifeless material</li>
        <li>Glass-filled or diffusion-treated stones</li>
        <li>Selection based only on dramatic colour</li>
        <li>Stones without certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Purple Sapphire, suitability matters more than visual intensity.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'padparadscha' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--padparadscha">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural coexistence of pink and orange tones</li>
        <li>No dominant single colour</li>
        <li>Soft, balanced glow (not harsh or artificial)</li>
        <li>Clear origin and treatment disclosure</li>
        <li>Verified third-party certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Stones that appear clearly pink or clearly orange</li>
        <li>Artificially bright or overly saturated tones</li>
        <li>Diffusion-treated or colour-enhanced stones</li>
        <li>Purchases without certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Certification Must Confirm:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural Sapphire</li>
        <li>Colour description</li>
        <li>Treatment status</li>
        <li>Verifiable report number</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Padparadscha is defined by precision — not approximation.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'white-coral' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--white_coral">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>White Coral is one of the most imitated gemstones in the market.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural creamy white or soft ivory tones</li>
        <li>Dense, compact feel (not lightweight)</li>
        <li>Smooth but organic surface finish</li>
        <li>Subtle natural variation</li>
        <li>Verified Italian or Japanese origin</li>
        <li>Clear authenticity disclosure or certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Stark white, artificial-looking pieces</li>
        <li>Plastic-like lightweight feel</li>
        <li>Overly uniform, ceramic-like surfaces</li>
        <li>Glossy synthetic shine</li>
        <li>Resin-filled or reconstructed coral</li>
        <li>Bone or composite substitutes</li>
        <li>“Too large, too cheap” offers</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Always Confirm:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural, organic sea coral</li>
        <li>Not dyed, reconstructed, or resin-filled</li>
        <li>Origin disclosure</li>
        <li>Certification or authenticity assurance</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If it looks too perfect, too white, or too cheap — pause. Authenticity matters more than appearance.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'moonstone' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--moonstone">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Moonstone is commonly imitated using glass or synthetic materials.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Visible internal glow (adularescence)</li>
        <li>Soft, shifting light beneath the surface</li>
        <li>Milky white, cream, or soft grey tones</li>
        <li>Balanced transparency</li>
        <li>Natural, non-uniform appearance</li>
        <li>Verified certification or seller disclosure</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Flat stones with no internal glow</li>
        <li>Plastic-like or artificial shine</li>
        <li>Overly bright, unnatural appearance</li>
        <li>No disclosure of treatment or origin</li>
        <li>Unverified “astrological” claims</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Certification Should Confirm:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural Moonstone (Feldspar)</li>
        <li>Treatment status</li>
        <li>No artificial colour enhancement</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Moonstone, the glow reveals authenticity.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'malachite' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--malachite">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Malachite is widely imitated using synthetic or composite materials.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Organic, flowing natural banding</li>
        <li>Variation in stripe thickness and tone</li>
        <li>Deep, layered green colour</li>
        <li>Solid, dense feel</li>
        <li>Natural polish that highlights internal patterns</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Perfectly repetitive or artificial patterns</li>
        <li>Flat, overly bright green colour</li>
        <li>Lightweight, plastic-like feel</li>
        <li>Dyed resin composites</li>
        <li>Powdered malachite bonded with adhesives</li>
        <li>Synthetic or imitation materials</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Malachite, pattern reveals authenticity.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'sphatik' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--sphatik">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Sphatik is often confused with glass or synthetic crystal due to its transparency.</p>
        <p>Choose pieces with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural transparency with slight internal character</li>
        <li>Cool, dense feel (not lightweight like glass)</li>
        <li>Smooth polish and balanced form</li>
        <li>Clear disclosure of natural quartz origin</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Perfectly flawless, “too-clean” pieces (may be synthetic or glass)</li>
        <li>Lightweight or warm-feeling material</li>
        <li>Artificially enhanced or misrepresented crystal</li>
        <li>No authenticity disclosure</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Certification (Recommended for Premium Pieces):</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural Quartz / Rock Crystal</li>
        <li>No artificial colouring</li>
        <li>Treatment disclosure</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Sphatik, authenticity matters more than perfection.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'blue-topaz' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--blue_topaz">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Blue Topaz requires transparency in selection and disclosure.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Preferred shade (Sky, Swiss, or London Blue)</li>
        <li>Clean transparency</li>
        <li>Good brilliance and light reflection</li>
        <li>Well-proportioned cutting</li>
        <li>Clear treatment disclosure</li>
        <li>Certification for higher-value pieces</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Poorly cut stones with dull appearance</li>
        <li>Cloudy or lifeless material</li>
        <li>Undisclosed or unclear treatments</li>
        <li>Sellers claiming untreated blue without clarity</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Blue Topaz, honesty matters as much as beauty.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'iolite' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--iolite">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Iolite requires careful selection to ensure quality and authenticity.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Rich blue to violet-blue tones</li>
        <li>Visible pleochroism</li>
        <li>Good transparency</li>
        <li>Balanced, well-executed cut</li>
        <li>Natural internal character</li>
        <li>Verified certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Dull grey or washed-out stones</li>
        <li>Glass or synthetic imitations</li>
        <li>Lifeless stones with no depth</li>
        <li>Misleading comparisons to Blue Sapphire</li>
        <li>Stones without certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Iolite should feel deep, calm, and quietly alive.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'natural-zircon' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--natural_zircon">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Most Important:</p>
        <p>Natural Zircon ≠ Cubic Zirconia</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural Zircon = genuine gemstone</li>
        <li>Cubic Zirconia (CZ) = lab-made imitation</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Verified Natural Zircon certification</li>
        <li>Strong, lively brilliance</li>
        <li>Good transparency (eye-clean appearance)</li>
        <li>Clear treatment disclosure</li>
        <li>Well-cut proportions</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Extremely cheap “Zircon” without proof</li>
        <li>Cubic Zirconia sold as natural</li>
        <li>Cloudy or lifeless stones</li>
        <li>No certification</li>
        <li>Vague or unclear claims</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Authenticity matters more than appearance.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'star-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--star_sapphire">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Star Sapphire is frequently misrepresented in the market.</p>
        <p>Always check:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>The star moves under light</li>
        <li>Multiple lighting conditions (not just spotlight)</li>
        <li>Certification confirming natural sapphire + natural asterism</li>
        <li>Treatment disclosure (heated/unheated acceptable if declared)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Synthetic star sapphires</li>
        <li>Painted or surface-applied stars</li>
        <li>Diffusion-treated or glass-filled stones</li>
        <li>Stones without certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If the star does not move naturally, it is not genuine.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'garnet' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--garnet">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Because Garnet exists in multiple varieties, clarity in selection is essential.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Deep, even colour appropriate to the variety</li>
        <li>Good natural brilliance</li>
        <li>Clear identification of Garnet type (Almandine, Pyrope, Rhodolite, Spessartite, Tsavorite, etc.)</li>
        <li>Suitable transparency for the variety</li>
        <li>Verified certification for higher-value stones</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Artificial or overly bright colour</li>
        <li>Poor or unclear variety disclosure</li>
        <li>Heavy inclusions that affect appearance</li>
        <li>High-value stones without certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Certification Should Confirm:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural Garnet</li>
        <li>Correct variety classification</li>
        <li>No undisclosed treatments</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Garnet, variety matters as much as colour.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'peridot' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--peridot">
        <p>Ensure the stone is certified natural. Be aware that green glass or synthetic material is sometimes sold as Peridot. Insist on a third-party lab certificate confirming identity and any treatments.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'tiger-eye' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--tiger_eye">
        <p>Ensure the stone is natural Tiger's Eye and not dyed or heat-treated to alter colour. A strong, sharp eye and natural golden-brown colour are signs of quality. Request lab certification for premium pieces.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'agate' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--agate">
        <p>Natural Agate should show genuine banding. Many commercial agates are dyed to enhance colour — this should always be disclosed. Request certification for premium pieces.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'chalcedony' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--chalcedony">
        <p>Some chalcedony is dyed or heat-treated. Natural colour should be disclosed on certification. Blue chalcedony from Namibia or Turkey is among the most valued. Request third-party certification for premium pieces.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'peach-sapphire' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--peach_sapphire">
        <h3>Buyer Awareness — Before You Buy</h3>
        <p>Peach Sapphire rewards discernment over impulse.</p>
        <p>Choose stones with:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Even, well-balanced peach tones</li>
        <li>Warm apricot or honey hues</li>
        <li>Soft glow in both natural and indoor light</li>
        <li>Good clarity without dullness</li>
        <li>Balanced, well-proportioned cut</li>
        <li>Clear treatment disclosure</li>
        <li>Verified third-party certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Avoid:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Brown, grey, or muddy tones</li>
        <li>Extremely pale stones with no presence</li>
        <li>Poor cuts that reduce brilliance</li>
        <li>Surface coatings or undisclosed treatments</li>
        <li>Stones without certification</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Certification Should Confirm:</p>
        <ul>
        <li>Natural Sapphire</li>
        <li>Treatment status</li>
        <li>Verifiable report number</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In Peach Sapphire, harmony matters more than size.</p>
      </div>
    {%- else -%}
      <div class="gem-tab"><p>Always insist on third-party lab certification. Ensure all treatments are fully disclosed.</p></div>
  {%- endcase -%}

